medium under the control of the recipient but, rather, gives the recipient access to a medium under the supplier’s control on which the data is stored. Illustration: 41. Car manufacturer S con- ducts intensive research on the development of new car models, collecting vast amounts of test data on various prototypes and their components. The data would enable engine manufacturer R to learn bet- ter how its engines operate and how they can be improved. S is willing to enter into a contract with R that would enable R to obtain that ben- efit but, in light of the vast invest- ment made by S into the research and the risk that any data trans- ferred to R will be passed on to com- petitors or hacked by third parties, S is reluctant to transfer the test data to R. Rather, the parties agree that R will have access to a defined class of test data on S’s servers. The contract effectuating this agree- Principle 8 ALI-ELI PRINCIPLES FOR A DATA ECONOMY"